  "question": {
    "text": "A laser light of wavelength <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>660</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#8201;</mi><mi>nm</mi></math> is used to weld Retina detachment. If a laser pulse of width <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>60</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#8201;</mi><mi>ms</mi></math> and power <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">0</mi><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>kW</mi></math> is used, the approximate number of photons in the pulse are (Take Planck&#39;s Constant, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6.62</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#8722;</mo><mn>34</mn></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#8201;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mo>)</mo></math>",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "zmHZ6",
      "text": "<math><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>22</mn></mrow></msup></math>",
